Java is a statically typed, class-based programming language and runtime platform designed around portability, safety, tooling, and a large standard library. Java source is normally compiled into JVM bytecode rather than directly into one operating system's machine code. A compatible Java Virtual Machine then executes that bytecode.

The important distinction is between the **language** and the **platform**. The language defines syntax and semantics such as classes, interfaces, generics, exceptions, and pattern matching. The platform supplies the JVM, standard libraries, command-line tools, garbage collection, monitoring facilities, and a mature ecosystem.

Java is widely used for backend services, enterprise systems, data processing, financial software, developer tools, and high-throughput server applications.

```java
public class HelloJava {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
}
```

A Java program is organized around types such as classes, interfaces, enums, and records. A conventional executable application has a `main` method. The usual entry point is `public static void main(String[] args)`.

`public` makes the method accessible to the launcher, `static` means the JVM can invoke it without creating an instance, `void` means it returns no value, and `String[] args` receives command-line arguments.

Java identifiers are case-sensitive. Package names are conventionally lowercase, classes use PascalCase, methods and variables use camelCase, and constants commonly use `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`.

```java
package com.example.app;

public class Application {
    private static final int DEFAULT_PORT = 8080;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Starting on port " + DEFAULT_PORT);
    }
}
```

Java has eight primitive types: `byte`, `short`, `int`, `long`, `float`, `double`, `char`, and `boolean`. Primitive variables hold values according to the language's rules. Reference variables hold references to objects, arrays, or `null`.

`int` is the usual integral type. Use `long` for larger integer ranges, `double` for general floating-point calculations, and `boolean` for true/false state. `char` represents a UTF-16 code unit.

```java
int count = 42;
long population = 8_000_000_000L;
double price = 19.95;
boolean active = true;
char grade = 'A';

String name = "Suriya";
int[] scores = {90, 85, 95};
```

Wrapper classes such as `Integer`, `Long`, and `Boolean` represent primitive values as objects and are required in many generic APIs.

Java performs some conversions automatically when they are safe, such as widening `int` to `long`. Narrowing conversions require an explicit cast and can lose information.

```java
int n = 10;
long big = n;
int smaller = (int) big;

Integer boxed = n;
int value = boxed;
```

Boxing is convenient but creates object values when an object representation is needed. It can also introduce `NullPointerException` during unboxing if a wrapper is `null`.

Numeric promotion can produce surprising results. `5 / 2` is `2` because both operands are integers; `5 / 2.0` is `2.5`.

Java supports arithmetic, comparison, logical, bitwise, shift, assignment, conditional, and increment/decrement operators.

```java
int a = 10;
int b = 3;

int sum = a + b;
int remainder = a % b;
boolean result = a > b && b > 0;
int max = a > b ? a : b;
```

`&&` and `||` are short-circuiting operators. `==` compares primitive values directly but compares references by identity when both operands are references. Use `.equals()` for logical value equality when the type defines it appropriately.

Java provides `if/else`, `switch`, `for`, enhanced `for`, `while`, and `do-while`. Modern Java also supports switch expressions, which can return a value.

```java
int score = 82;

String result = switch (score / 10) {
    case 10, 9 -> "Excellent";
    case 8 -> "Good";
    case 7 -> "Average";
    default -> "Needs improvement";
};

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

Prefer the construct that expresses intent most clearly. Enhanced `for` is ideal for straightforward iteration. Streams are useful when the operation is naturally a data transformation pipeline; they are not automatically better than a loop.

A class defines state and behavior; an object is a runtime instance. Constructors establish valid initial state. Fields should normally be private, with behavior exposed through methods rather than unrestricted mutation.

```java
public final class User {
    private final String id;
    private String displayName;

    public User(String id, String displayName) {
        this.id = id;
        this.displayName = displayName;
    }

    public String id() { return id; }
    public String displayName() { return displayName; }
}
```

Favor constructors that prevent invalid states. If a field must never change after construction, make it `final`.

Encapsulation hides implementation details behind a stable API. Inheritance should model a genuine "is-a" relationship. Polymorphism allows code to depend on an abstraction while the runtime object determines the implementation.

```java
interface PaymentProcessor {
    void pay(long cents);
}

final class CardPayment implements PaymentProcessor {
    public void pay(long cents) {
        System.out.println("Card: " + cents);
    }
}

final class Checkout {
    private final PaymentProcessor processor;

    Checkout(PaymentProcessor processor) {
        this.processor = processor;
    }

    void complete(long cents) {
        processor.pay(cents);
    }
}
```

`Checkout` does not care whether payment uses a card, wallet, or test double. This is dependency inversion through an interface.

An abstract class can hold shared state and implementation while preventing direct instantiation. An interface primarily defines a capability or contract and can contain default, static, and private methods.

A class can implement multiple interfaces but extend only one class.

```java
interface Auditable {
    String actor();
}

abstract class BaseCommand {
    private final long createdAt = System.currentTimeMillis();
    long createdAt() { return createdAt; }
}

final class CreateUserCommand extends BaseCommand implements Auditable {
    public String actor() { return "system"; }
}
```

Modern Java provides concise tools for domain modeling. A **record** is ideal for transparent, mostly immutable data carriers. A **sealed type** restricts which types may extend or implement an abstraction. An enum models a fixed set of named constants.

```java
public record Point(int x, int y) {}

public sealed interface Result
        permits Success, Failure {}

public record Success(String value) implements Result {}
public record Failure(String reason) implements Result {}

enum Status {
    NEW, PROCESSING, COMPLETED, FAILED
}
```

Records generate accessors, a canonical constructor, `equals`, `hashCode`, and `toString`. They do not make referenced objects deeply immutable.

A method should perform one coherent operation and expose a clear contract. Java passes arguments **by value**. For object arguments, the copied value is the reference itself.

```java
static int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

static int sum(int... values) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int value : values) total += value;
    return total;
}
```

Varargs are compiled as an array parameter and must be the final parameter.

Arrays are fixed-size objects with zero-based indexing.

```java
int[] values = new int[3];
values[0] = 10;

int[] scores = {90, 80, 95};

int[][] matrix = {
    {1, 2},
    {3, 4}
};
```

Java's multidimensional arrays are arrays of arrays, so rows can have different lengths. Arrays are efficient for indexed access, but collections are usually better when size or data-structure semantics vary.

`String` is immutable. Operations such as concatenation produce a new string rather than changing the existing instance.

```java
String name = "Java";
String upper = name.toUpperCase();

StringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    builder.append(i).append(',');
}

String result = builder.toString();
```

Repeated concatenation in a large loop can create unnecessary temporary strings. `StringBuilder` is generally appropriate for incremental construction in one thread.

The Collections Framework provides reusable data structures and algorithms. `List` preserves order and allows duplicates. `Set` models uniqueness. `Map` associates keys with values.

```java
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
names.add("A");
names.add("B");

Set<String> unique = new HashSet<>(names);

Map<String, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
counts.merge("java", 1, Integer::sum);
```

`ArrayList` is usually the default list. `HashSet` and `HashMap` provide average constant-time lookup under good hashing behavior. `LinkedHashMap` preserves insertion order, while `TreeMap` keeps keys sorted.

Queues represent ordered work. `ArrayDeque` is a strong general-purpose implementation for stack and queue operations. `PriorityQueue` orders elements according to priority.

```java
Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
stack.push("first");
stack.push("second");
System.out.println(stack.pop());

Queue<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
queue.offer("job-1");
queue.offer("job-2");
System.out.println(queue.poll());

PriorityQueue<Integer> priorities = new PriorityQueue<>();
priorities.offer(50);
priorities.offer(10);
System.out.println(priorities.poll());
```

A priority queue's internal array is not globally sorted; it maintains a heap property sufficient to retrieve the highest- or lowest-priority element efficiently.

Generics provide compile-time type safety without repeated casts.

```java
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
names.add("Java");

static double sum(List<? extends Number> values) {
    double total = 0;
    for (Number value : values) {
        total += value.doubleValue();
    }
    return total;
}
```

`? extends T` is useful when a structure is primarily a producer of `T`. `? super T` is useful when it consumes `T`. This is commonly remembered as **PECS: Producer Extends, Consumer Super**.

Generic type information is largely erased at runtime.

Java separates natural ordering from custom ordering. `Comparable<T>` defines a type's natural order; `Comparator<T>` supplies an external ordering strategy.

```java
record Person(String name, int age) {}

List<Person> people = new ArrayList<>();
people.sort(
    Comparator.comparingInt(Person::age)
              .thenComparing(Person::name)
);
```

Iterators allow controlled traversal and, where supported, removal. The enhanced `for` loop uses an iterator for iterable collections.

Comparators should obey their contract; inconsistent ordering can cause unpredictable sorting behavior.

Java exceptions derive from `Throwable`. `Error` generally represents serious JVM/runtime conditions. `Exception` represents conditions applications may handle; `RuntimeException` and subclasses are unchecked.

Checked exceptions must be caught or declared. Neither category should be chosen mechanically; decide based on whether the API contract benefits from requiring callers to explicitly handle a recoverable condition.

```java
try {
    Files.readString(Path.of("config.txt"));
} catch (IOException e) {
    throw new ConfigurationException("Cannot read configuration", e);
}
```

Exceptions propagate up the call stack until a matching handler is found. `finally` is intended for cleanup, although try-with-resources is usually safer for resources.

```java
try {
    process();
} catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
    System.err.println("Invalid input: " + e.getMessage());
} finally {
    auditCompletion();
}
```

Catch specific exceptions rather than catching `Exception` everywhere. If an exception cannot be handled meaningfully at the current layer, propagate it with useful context.

Resources such as files, sockets, and database statements often implement `AutoCloseable`. Try-with-resources guarantees closure at the end of the statement's scope.

```java
try (BufferedReader reader =
         Files.newBufferedReader(Path.of("app.log"))) {
    String line;
    while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
        System.out.println(line);
    }
} catch (IOException e) {
    throw new RuntimeException("Unable to read log", e);
}
```

If both the body and `close()` throw, Java preserves the close failure as a suppressed exception.

Domain-specific exceptions communicate failure meaning to callers.

```java
public final class InsufficientBalanceException
        extends RuntimeException {
    public InsufficientBalanceException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}
```

At service boundaries, convert low-level failures into domain-level errors when that improves the API contract. Avoid leaking secrets, SQL statements with sensitive values, access tokens, or personal data into exception messages and logs.

The JVM specification describes runtime data areas such as the heap, JVM stacks, method area, program counter registers, and native method stacks.

The **heap** is shared among threads and stores objects and arrays. Each thread has its own JVM stack containing frames for active method invocations.

```java
static int calculate(int x) {
    int doubled = x * 2;
    return doubled + 1;
}
```

Each call creates a frame on the executing thread's stack. Object values created by application code are generally managed on the heap.

A lambda is a compact implementation of a functional interface: an interface with one abstract method.

```java
Predicate<String> nonEmpty = value -> !value.isBlank();
Function<String, Integer> length = String::length;
Consumer<String> log = System.out::println;
```

Common functional interfaces include `Predicate<T>`, `Function<T,R>`, `Consumer<T>`, and `Supplier<T>`. Primitive-specialized forms can reduce boxing in hot numeric code.

A lambda should generally be small. Complex behavior is often clearer as a named method.

Modern Java date-time APIs separate date, local date-time, instant, offset, and time zone.

```java
Instant now = Instant.now();
LocalDate today = LocalDate.now();
ZonedDateTime india =
    ZonedDateTime.now(ZoneId.of("Asia/Kolkata"));

Duration duration = Duration.between(
    now, now.plusSeconds(30)
);
```

Use `Instant` for machine-oriented points in time, `LocalDate` for a date without a zone, and `ZonedDateTime` when location matters.

For financial systems, `BigDecimal` is generally preferred when decimal arithmetic must be exact.

NIO.2 provides `Path` and `Files` for modern file operations.

```java
Path path = Path.of("data", "users.txt");

Files.writeString(
    path,
    "Alice\nBob\n",
    StandardOpenOption.CREATE,
    StandardOpenOption.TRUNCATE_EXISTING
);

String content = Files.readString(path);
```

Byte streams handle binary data; character readers/writers handle text. Buffered APIs reduce the cost of many small operations. For large files, stream line-by-line instead of loading the entire file.

NIO channels provide a lower-level I/O abstraction. Buffers hold data being transferred between application code and channels.

```java
try (FileChannel channel =
         FileChannel.open(Path.of("data.bin"), StandardOpenOption.READ)) {

    ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocate(4096);
    int read = channel.read(buffer);
    System.out.println("Read: " + read);
}
```

A `ByteBuffer` has position, limit, capacity, and remaining bytes. Calling `flip()` changes a buffer from writing mode to reading mode.

A Java thread represents an execution path. Production applications generally benefit from executors that manage task scheduling and lifecycle.

```java
Runnable task = () ->
    System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName());

Thread thread = new Thread(task);
thread.start();
```

Calling `start()` creates a new execution path; calling `run()` directly is an ordinary method call on the current thread.

Shared mutable state is where concurrency becomes difficult. Ask who owns state, who can mutate it, and what synchronization establishes visibility and ordering.

`synchronized` provides mutual exclusion and memory-visibility guarantees. `ReentrantLock` offers additional features such as timed acquisition.

```java
private final Object lock = new Object();
private int count;

void increment() {
    synchronized (lock) {
        count++;
    }
}

int getCount() {
    synchronized (lock) {
        return count;
    }
}
```

A data race occurs when concurrent access includes a write without appropriate synchronization. The Java Memory Model defines happens-before relationships.

`volatile` provides visibility and ordering guarantees but does not make compound operations such as `count++` atomic.

Executors separate task submission from thread management. `CompletableFuture` composes asynchronous stages.

```java
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);

CompletableFuture<String> future =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> loadUser(), pool)
        .thenApply(User::name);

future.thenAccept(System.out::println);
```

Choose executor sizes based on workload. CPU-bound and blocking I/O workloads usually need different strategies. Always define shutdown behavior for owned executors.

Virtual threads are lightweight Java threads designed to make high-concurrency blocking workloads easier to express.

```java
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    Future<String> future =
        executor.submit(() -> fetchRemoteData());
    System.out.println(future.get());
}
```

Virtual threads do not make CPU-bound work faster. They improve scalability for workloads where many tasks spend time waiting. Concurrency still needs limits around databases, APIs, queues, and other external resources.

Common retention sources include unbounded maps, static collections, caches without eviction, listener registrations, thread locals, and accidentally retained object graphs.

```java
static final Map<String, byte[]> cache = new HashMap<>();

void add(String id, byte[] data) {
    cache.put(id, data);
}
```

A heap dump reveals retained memory. Allocation profiling identifies where objects are created rapidly. GC logs and JFR show collection behavior and allocation pressure.

Ask: What is growing? Which objects are retained? Who retains them? Is the growth expected? What is the live set after GC?

Switch expressions allow a `switch` to produce a value and support arrow labels that avoid accidental fall-through.

```java
String category = switch (status) {
    case NEW -> "pending";
    case PROCESSING -> "active";
    case COMPLETED -> "done";
    case FAILED -> "error";
};
```

When multiple statements are needed before producing a value, `yield` can return the result from a block. Exhaustiveness is especially useful with enums and sealed hierarchies.

Pattern matching reduces repetitive type checks and casts.

```java
if (value instanceof String text && !text.isBlank()) {
    System.out.println(text.trim());
}
```

Modern Java also extends pattern matching to switch in supported releases, making handling of sealed hierarchies more concise.

Use pattern matching to clarify domain logic rather than replacing good object design with large type-dispatch blocks.

Records are concise data carriers. Sealed types restrict extension to a known set.

```java
sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rectangle {}

record Circle(double radius) implements Shape {}
record Rectangle(double width, double height) implements Shape {}
```

Together, these features help model closed domains where the variants are intentionally known.

Reflection lets code inspect classes, methods, fields, constructors, and annotations at runtime.

```java
Class<?> type = User.class;

for (var method : type.getDeclaredMethods()) {
    System.out.println(method.getName());
}
```

Frameworks use reflection for dependency injection, serialization, ORM mapping, testing, and plugin systems. Reflection can weaken compile-time guarantees and complicate encapsulation, performance analysis, and native-image compatibility.

Use reflection at framework boundaries rather than spreading it through ordinary business logic.

Annotations attach metadata to declarations. Frameworks can define custom annotations and inspect them at compile time or runtime.

```java
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
public @interface Audited {
    String action();
}
```

Retention controls whether metadata is available in source, class files, or runtime reflection. Annotation processing can generate code during compilation and avoid some runtime reflection.

Java can create dynamic proxies around interfaces, enabling cross-cutting behavior such as logging, metrics, authorization, or transaction interception. Service loading provides a standard plugin discovery mechanism.

```java
ServiceLoader<MyPlugin> plugins =
    ServiceLoader.load(MyPlugin.class);

for (MyPlugin plugin : plugins) {
    plugin.start();
}
```

These mechanisms are useful for extensibility but introduce indirection that should be documented.

A module declares dependencies and exported packages.

```text
module com.example.orders {
    requires java.net.http;
    exports com.example.orders.api;
}
```

`exports` controls package access across modules. `opens` is relevant when reflective access to otherwise encapsulated packages is required.

Packages organize names; modules define a larger dependency and encapsulation boundary.

Unit tests isolate a small behavior and should be fast and deterministic. Integration tests verify interactions with real components such as databases, message brokers, HTTP servers, or filesystems.

```java
@Test
void calculatesTotal() {
    Cart cart = new Cart();
    cart.add(new Item("Book", 500));

    assertEquals(500, cart.total());
}
```

A healthy test strategy uses the cheapest test that provides useful confidence while maintaining enough realistic integration coverage to catch wiring and infrastructure failures.

A test double replaces a real dependency. A stub supplies predetermined answers, a fake provides a lightweight working implementation, and a mock is commonly used to verify interactions.

Over-mocking makes tests brittle when they verify implementation details.

```java
UserRepository repository = new InMemoryUserRepository();
UserService service = new UserService(repository);

assertTrue(service.exists("u-1"));
```

Prefer real lightweight implementations where practical. Mock external systems when setup is expensive or unreliable.

JDBC provides a standard API for relational database access. A connection pool reuses database connections rather than creating a new connection for every request.

```java
try (Connection connection = dataSource.getConnection();
     PreparedStatement statement =
         connection.prepareStatement(
             "select id, name from users where id = ?")) {

    statement.setString(1, userId);

    try (ResultSet rs = statement.executeQuery()) {
        if (rs.next()) {
            return rs.getString("name");
        }
    }
}
```

Always use parameterized queries. Pool size should account for database capacity and query latency; making it huge can overload the database.

A transaction groups database operations into an atomic unit. Isolation controls how concurrent transactions observe each other's changes.

```java
connection.setAutoCommit(false);

try {
    debitAccount(connection, fromId, amount);
    creditAccount(connection, toId, amount);
    connection.commit();
} catch (Exception e) {
    connection.rollback();
    throw e;
}
```

Transaction boundaries should usually live at a service/use-case layer rather than being scattered across repository methods.

SOLID is a set of design heuristics. Single Responsibility encourages cohesive responsibilities. Open/Closed encourages extension without repeatedly modifying stable behavior. Liskov Substitution focuses on substitutability. Interface Segregation avoids irrelevant dependencies. Dependency Inversion encourages high-level policy to depend on abstractions.

```java
interface NotificationSender {
    void send(String recipient, String message);
}

final class OrderService {
    private final NotificationSender sender;

    OrderService(NotificationSender sender) {
        this.sender = sender;
    }
}
```

The value is reduced coupling and easier testing, not creating an interface for every class.

Common patterns include Factory, Builder, Strategy, Adapter, Decorator, Observer, Template Method, Command, and State.

```java
interface PricingStrategy {
    long price(Order order);
}

final class RegularPricing implements PricingStrategy {
    public long price(Order order) {
        return order.subtotal();
    }
}
```

Strategy is polymorphism used to select interchangeable behavior. Builder helps construct complex immutable objects. Adapter translates one interface into another. Decorator adds behavior without modifying the wrapped implementation.

A good domain model captures meaningful business invariants. Value objects are useful when a concept has identity-independent rules such as money, email addresses, coordinates, or date ranges.

```java
public final class Money {
    private final BigDecimal amount;
    private final Currency currency;

    public Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {
        this.amount = amount;
        this.currency = currency;
    }
}
```

Avoid both extremes: scattering business rules everywhere and turning every simple CRUD operation into an elaborate framework.

Treat all external input as untrusted: HTTP parameters, JSON bodies, headers, uploaded files, messages, and untrusted external data.

```java
if (username == null || !username.matches("[A-Za-z0-9_]{3,32}")) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid username");
}
```

Validation is not only about security. It protects downstream components from malformed data and makes API behavior predictable. Output encoding must be appropriate for its context.

Use standard cryptographic libraries rather than implementing algorithms yourself. Passwords should be stored using dedicated password hashing rather than reversible encryption or fast hashes.

Secrets such as API keys and database passwords should come from secure secret management rather than source control.

```java
// Conceptual:
// secret = secretManager.get("payments/api-key");
// client.authenticate(secret);
```

Encryption, hashing, signing, and key derivation are different operations with different purposes.

Separate code from environment-specific configuration. Configuration can come from environment variables, mounted files, secret stores, or deployment configuration.

```java
String port = System.getenv().getOrDefault("PORT", "8080");
int httpPort = Integer.parseInt(port);
```

Validate configuration during startup so failures happen early. Never print credentials or tokens while diagnosing configuration.
